    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8315\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill wilt allow for a restaurant, Bleu Nuk Bk, located at 1150\nNostrand Avenue, Brooklyn, New York, to obtain a full liquor license.\nThe bill will amend the ABC 200-foot Law which will authorize the issu-\ning of a retail liquor license to a business in the County of Kings,\nBrooklyn that is within a two-hundred-foot proximity of a place of\nworship.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 7 of section 64 of the alcoholic beverage control\nlaw is amended by adding a new paragraph (e-17) to read as follows:\n\ne-17) Notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph(a)of this subdivision,\nthe authority may issue a retail license for on-premises consumption for\na premises which shall be located within two hundred feet of a building\noccupied as a school, church, synagogue or other place of worship,\nprovided such premises constitute a premises for the sale of food or\nbeverages at retail for consumption on the premises located wholly with-\nin the boundaries of the Borough of Brooklyn, county of Kings, bounded\nand described as follows:\n\nALL THAT CERTAIN plot, piece or parcel of land, with the buildings and\nimprovements thereon erect, tying and being in the Borough of Brooklyn,\nCounty of Kings, City and State of New York, bounded and described as\nfollows:\n\nBEGINNING at the corner formed by the intersection of the westerly side\n16 of Nostrand Avenue with the northerly side of Rutland Road, RUNNING\nTHENCE northerly, along the westerly side of Nostrand Avenue, 20 feet,\n\nTHENCE westerly, parallel with Rutland Road and part of the distance\nthrough party wall 80 feet, THENCE southerly parallel with Nostrand\nAvenue, 20 feet to the northerly side of Rutland Road,\n\nTHENCE easterly along the northerly side of Rutland Road, 80 feet to the\n24 point or place of BEGINNING.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany urban communities across the State are facing an uphill battle when\nit comes to maintaining, developing, and restoring their historic commu-\nnity areas. Many of these historic areas and districts have existing\ninfrastructures where schools, theatres, universities and churches are\nin close proximity to various store fronts and restaurants. Flatbush,\nthe neighborhood where Bleu Nuk Bk Restaurant is located, is a destina-\ntion for culture and entertainment. This authentic Caribbean establish-\nment has served as the cultural mecca for Caribbean cuisine in Flatbush\nBrooklyn. Because of the existing ABC 200-foot law, this establishment\nhas been restricted in fully serving its customers which makes it a\nchallenge to compete, thus impacting the future of business. Further-\nmore, the religious institution which is within 200-feet, Beulah 'United\nChurch of God, has expressed that they have no objections to the request\nto apply for a full liquor license. An exemption to the ABC 200-foot Law\nwould allow 1150 Nostrand Avenue to operate with a full liquor license\nthey will be able to meet the needs of the growing Caribbean community\nand attract more patrons to the new cultural center of Brooklyn by\noffering traditional Caribbean cuisines that incl ude various types of\nliquor as well as cultural alcohol beverages.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\n\nNone.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
